# README — Source Data: SHAP Beeswarm Plots (Binary Classification Models)

## Overview
This file contains the SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) values and corresponding
feature values underlying the beeswarm plots shown for each pairwise binary
classification model distinguishing the three immune-reservoir endotypes (All Low,
All High, Mixed) identified by multi-omics clustering in the 2000HIV cohort
(NCT03994835).

## Models covered
Three pairwise binary classification comparisons are included, one set of sheets
per comparison:
- All Low vs Mixed
- Mixed vs All High
- All Low vs All High

## Sheet contents
Each comparison has three sheets:

### [Comparison]_SHAP
Rows = training samples used for that binary model. Columns:
- **Sample_ID:** sample identifier, consistent with IDs used elsewhere in the
  manuscript's source data (e.g., the multi-omics heatmap Endotype_Label sheet).
- **[Feature columns]:** the SHAP value for each feature, for each sample, as
  computed for that model. This is the value plotted along the x-axis of the
  beeswarm plot.

### [Comparison]_Features
Same samples and same features, in the same row/column order as the corresponding
_SHAP sheet. Columns:
- **Sample_ID:** as above.
- **[Feature columns]:** the processed feature value used as model input for that
  sample. This is the value that determines point color (low-to-high) in the
  beeswarm plot.

Note: the _SHAP and _Features sheets must be read together — a beeswarm plot point
is defined by a (SHAP value, feature value) pair for a given sample and feature.

### [Comparison]_Summary
One row per feature, ranked by importance (Rank 1 = top of the beeswarm plot).
- **Rank:** feature ranking by mean absolute SHAP value, matching the top-to-bottom
  order of features in the beeswarm plot.
- **Mean_Abs_SHAP:** mean absolute SHAP value across samples; determines feature
  ranking/ordering in the beeswarm plot.
- **Mean_SHAP:** mean signed SHAP value; indicates overall directional effect.
- **Min_SHAP / Max_SHAP:** range of SHAP values across samples, corresponding to
  the horizontal spread of points for that feature in the plot.
- **Min_Feature_Value / Max_Feature_Value:** range of the underlying (processed)
  feature value, corresponding to the color range (low-to-high) shown for that
  feature's points in the plot.

This sheet is provided as a convenience summary to allow a quick cross-check
against the published figure; the full per-sample values in the corresponding
_SHAP and _Features sheets are the primary source data.

## Data processing
- Feature values shown are post-preprocessing (imputation, encoding, scaling as
  applicable), i.e., the exact input passed to the trained classification models,
  not raw/original-scale measurements.
- SHAP values were computed using `shap.Explainer(trained_model, X)` followed by
  `explainer(X)` (SHAP Python package), with the full processed feature matrix
  passed explicitly as background data (interventional feature perturbation).
  This matches the exact computation used to generate the published beeswarm
  figures.
- Only features used as input to each specific binary model are included in that
  model's sheets; feature sets may differ slightly between comparisons due to
  model-specific feature selection (e.g., variance inflation factor–based
  multicollinearity reduction).

## Quality checks performed
Before export, each comparison's per-feature Min_Feature_Value and
Max_Feature_Value (in the _Summary sheet) were checked to confirm they are not
identical across all features, which would indicate a global (array-wide) scaling
artifact rather than genuine per-feature ranges.

## Relationship to other source data
Sample-level cluster/endotype assignments corresponding to these Sample_IDs are
also provided in the Endotype_Label sheet of the multi-omics heatmap source data
file. Raw (pre-processing) values for the underlying omics and clinical features
are deposited in the Radboud Data Repository (RDR); see the manuscript's Data
Availability statement for repository links.

